---
import LessonLayout      from '@/layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import LessonHeader      from '@/components/LessonHeader.astro';
import LessonCallout     from '@/components/LessonCallout.astro';
import LessonDiagram     from '@/components/LessonDiagram.astro';
import LessonChecklist   from '@/components/LessonChecklist.astro';
import LessonQuiz        from '@/components/LessonQuiz.astro';
import DeepDive          from '@/components/DeepDive.astro';
import LessonMindMap     from '@/components/LessonMindMap.astro';
import LessonToc         from '@/components/LessonToc.astro';
import SourceBadge       from '@/components/SourceBadge.astro';
import NotebookView      from '@/components/NotebookView.astro';
import workflowNb        from '../../../../code/course-1/4.6-claims-workflow/claims_workflow.ipynb?raw';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const mindMap = `
# Exercise: Insurance Claims as a Workflow
## The goal
### Same claims, data and tools as the 4.5 agent
### Code fixes the steps; one LLM call
## Chain vs. workflow
### Chain: every step an LLM call
### Workflow: code steps + LLM where needed, with branches
## The steps
### Extract (LLM) → gate → retry with feedback
### Coverage → price → severity → route (code)
### Not covered → needs_review
## Results
### Same queues as the agent should reach
### 1 LLM call per claim vs. 4–5
## Limits
### Every new case is a new branch
### Too many branches → consider an agent step
`.trim();
---

<LessonLayout title="4.6 — Exercise: Insurance Claims as a Workflow">

  <LessonHeader
    num="4.6"
    title="Exercise: Insurance Claims as a Workflow"
    subtitle="The same claims and tools as the 4.5 agent, but code fixes the steps and the LLM is used once."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <LessonToc />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Goal <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>
      Lesson <a href={`${base}/lessons/0013-exercise-claim-agent`}>4.5</a> built insurance-claim triage as an agent and
      concluded that, for this job, a workflow would do better. This exercise builds that workflow, with the
      <strong>same claims, the same data and the same tools</strong> (coverage check, price table, routing rules),
      so the comparison is fair.
    </p>

    <p>
      The difference is who picks the steps. In 4.5 the model chose the next tool every turn. Here your code runs
      the steps in a fixed order, and the LLM is called <strong>once</strong>, for the only thing code can't do:
      read the claim text and say what happened and what's damaged.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Prompt Chain vs. Workflow <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>
      Lesson <a href={`${base}/lessons/0009-exercise-claim-triage`}>4.4</a> was also a fixed sequence, so what's
      different? In 4.4 every stage was an LLM call: extract, then assess severity and cost, then route. A prompt chain
      is a workflow whose steps are all LLM calls. This workflow mixes the two kinds of step: one LLM call where
      judgment is needed, and code everywhere else.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>4.4 prompt chain</th><th>4.6 workflow</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Steps</td><td>3 LLM calls in a line</td><td>1 LLM call, then 4 code steps</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Severity and cost</td><td>The model guesses them</td><td>Code: price table, then the cost band</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Not checked</td><td>Checked, with a branch to <code>needs_review</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gates</td><td>After every LLM stage</td><td>After the one LLM stage; code steps don't need one</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hail claim (C004)</td><td>Moderate, $3,000: passed every gate, wrong</td><td>Major, $5,450 → <code>total_loss</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      This table compares with 4.4 as the course built it, which had no policy data or price table, so part of the
      difference is information, not design. Lesson
      <a href={`${base}/lessons/0017-decision-guide-chain-workflow-agent`}>4.7</a> compares the designs fairly, with all
      three given the same tables.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Steps <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart TB
  F["📄 FNOL text"]:::in --> X["🤖 Extract (the one LLM call)<br/>incident type + damaged areas"]:::llm
  X --> G{{"🚦 Pydantic gate<br/>allowed types and areas?"}}:::gate
  G -.->|"❌ error as feedback<br/>(up to 3 tries)"| X
  G -.->|"❌ 3 failures"| R["👤 needs_review"]:::warn
  G -->|"✅ ClaimFacts"| C["🛠️ check_coverage"]:::code
  C -.->|"not covered"| R
  C -->|"covered"| P["🛠️ price_damage"]:::code
  P --> S["🧮 severity from the total"]:::code
  S --> Q["🛠️ route_claim"]:::code
  Q --> D["📥 glass · fast_track · material_damage · total_loss"]:::done

  classDef in   fill:#f1f5f9,color:#0f172a,stroke:#64748b,stroke-width:2px
  classDef llm  fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef code fill:#fef3c7,color:#78350f,stroke:#d97706,stroke-width:2px
  classDef gate fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
  classDef warn fill:#fee2e2,color:#7f1d1d,stroke:#dc2626,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Step</th><th>Who</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Extract</td><td>LLM</td><td>Returns <code>ClaimFacts</code>: claim ID, incident type, damaged areas (an area listed once per part, so two doors is <code>["door", "door"]</code>)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gate + retry</td><td>Code</td><td>Pydantic refuses anything outside the allowed lists; the error goes back to the model, up to 3 tries. This is the workflow's only feedback loop.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Check coverage</td><td>Code</td><td>The one branch: not covered, or the policy has lapsed → <code>needs_review</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Price the damage</td><td>Code</td><td>The price table from 4.5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Severity</td><td>Code</td><td>From the total: up to $1,000 Minor, up to $5,000 Moderate, above that Major</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Route</td><td>Code</td><td>4.4's routing rules</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      In 4.5 the agent chose a severity and the final gate checked it against the price. Here there's nothing to
      check: once the cost comes from a table, the severity follows from the cost bands, so code computes it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">What the Code Part Produces <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>
      Everything after extraction runs without a model. Given the facts a correct extraction returns, the notebook's
      "you play the extraction step" cell produces:
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Claim</th><th>Queue</th><th>Severity · cost</th><th>Steps after extraction</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>C001 windshield chip</td><td><code>glass</code></td><td>Minor · $300</td><td>coverage › price › route</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C002 parked, bumper + taillight</td><td><code>material_damage</code></td><td>Moderate · $1,250</td><td>coverage › price › route</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C003 collision, engine</td><td><code>total_loss</code></td><td>Major · $11,400</td><td>coverage › price › route</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C004 hail</td><td><code>total_loss</code></td><td>Major · $5,450</td><td>coverage › price › route</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C005 keyed doors</td><td><code>needs_review</code></td><td>not covered (vandalism needs comprehensive)</td><td>coverage</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C006 lapsed policy</td><td><code>needs_review</code></td><td>policy not active</td><td>coverage</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      These are the queues the 4.5 agent should reach, with one LLM call per claim instead of four or five, and the
      same steps every time. The only part that can vary between runs is the extraction, and it's gated.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Run It Yourself <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Setup:</strong> Colab's built-in model.</li>
      <li><strong>Data and tools:</strong> unchanged from 4.5.</li>
      <li><strong>The one LLM step:</strong> <code>ClaimFacts</code>, the extraction prompt, and the retry with feedback.</li>
      <li><strong>The workflow:</strong> <code>decide</code> (all code) and <code>triage</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>You play the extraction step:</strong> the results above, plus the gate refusing bad extractions. No LLM needed.</li>
      <li><strong>Run the workflow</strong> on the six claims, with the LLM calls counted.</li>
      <li><strong>Your turn:</strong> theft, injuries, and when the branches get too many.</li>
    </ol>

    <NotebookView notebook={workflowNb} path="code/course-1/4.6-claims-workflow/claims_workflow.ipynb" />

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="Running it in Colab">
      <p>
        <strong>Run the setup cell first.</strong> The notebook uses Colab's built-in models, no API key needed;
        the setup cell lists them, and uncommenting a <code>MODEL = ...</code> line there picks one. Then run the 4.5
        notebook on the same claims and compare <code>llm_calls</code> with the agent's steps.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Where the Workflow Runs Out <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <p>
      This workflow has one branch. Real claims bring more: a theft has nothing to price and needs a police report;
      an injury goes to a different team; a vague description needs photos before anything else. Each is another
      <code>if</code> in <code>decide</code>, and each needs the extraction to return one more field.
    </p>

    <p>
      That's fine for a handful of cases. When the branches keep multiplying, or the right next step depends on what
      the previous one found, it's time to consider an agent step for that part. Lesson
      <a href={`${base}/lessons/0017-decision-guide-chain-workflow-agent`}>4.7</a> compares all three designs and
      turns this into a decision guide.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Checklist <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <LessonChecklist
      title="Building a workflow with LLM steps:"
      items={[
        '<strong>Write the steps down first</strong>; if you can, a workflow is an option',
        '<strong>Use the LLM only where text needs reading or writing</strong>; everything else is code',
        '<strong>Extract into a typed model</strong> that has exactly the fields the code steps need',
        '<strong>Gate the LLM step and retry with the error</strong>, capped, then hand over to a person',
        '<strong>Compute what follows from data</strong> (severity from cost) instead of asking a model',
        '<strong>Branches are explicit</strong>: every early exit has a reason',
        '<strong>Record the steps</strong> so runs can be compared with other designs',
        '<strong>Watch the branch count</strong>: many special cases are a signal to consider an agent step',
      ]}
    />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Quick-check quiz <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h2>

    <LessonQuiz questions={[
      {
        q:       'What makes 4.6 a workflow rather than a prompt chain like 4.4?',
        options: ['Code runs most steps; the LLM is one step among them, with a branch', 'It uses Pydantic', 'It has no fixed order', 'The model picks the next step'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'A prompt chain is a workflow whose steps are all LLM calls. Here only extraction is.',
      },
      {
        q:       'Why does the workflow compute severity in code instead of asking the model?',
        options: ['The cost comes from the price table, and severity follows from the cost bands', 'The model cannot classify severity', 'Severity is not needed for routing', 'Pydantic computes it'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Once an input is grounded in data, whatever follows from it by rule belongs in code.',
      },
      {
        q:       'How many LLM calls does the workflow make for a claim when extraction passes the gate first time?',
        options: ['1', '2', '4–5', 'One per code step'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Only extraction calls the model. The 4.5 agent needs one call per tool call.',
      },
      {
        q:       'Which change would most push you toward an agent step?',
        options: ['Many new claim types whose next step depends on what earlier checks found', 'A new price in the table', 'A faster model', 'One more queue name'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Paths that vary with findings are where agents earn their cost.',
      },
    ]} />

  </div>

  <DeepDive
    sources="own exercise, building on lesson 4.4 (prompt chain) and lesson 4.5 (agent), same data and tools"
    tensions={[
      { a: 'Fixed steps (predictable, cheap, testable)', b: 'New cases need new code' },
      { a: 'One big extraction (one call)', b: 'More fields to get right in a single answer' },
      { a: 'Compute severity from cost (consistent)', b: 'Loses judgment the price table can’t express' },
    ]}
    questions={[
      {
        q:      'The workflow trusts the extraction completely: a missed damage area means a lower price. How would you reduce that risk without adding an agent?',
        hint:   'Where else could you check the extraction?',
        answer: '<p>Add a second check on the extraction: a rule cross-check in code (airbags deployed or "not drivable" in the text → at least Major; escalate if not), a second LLM call that only answers "is any damage in the text missing from this list?", or a sample of claims reviewed by people. All of these stay inside the workflow; they add steps, not autonomy.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'Severity used to be a judgment (the course’s heuristics: "multiple panels, structural"). Is computing it from cost a loss?',
        hint:   'What does the price table not know?',
        answer: '<p>Partly. The table knows typical repair costs, not structural damage, frame alignment or whether a car is worth repairing at all. For most claims the cost band is a good proxy; for the expensive tail it isn’t. A reasonable design keeps the code rule and adds a branch: claims near a band boundary, or with structural keywords, go to a person or get an LLM judgment step.</p>',
      },
    ]}
    pitfalls={[
      'Calling the model for steps that code can compute from data.',
      'Gating code steps as if they were LLM steps, and not gating the LLM step.',
      'An extraction model missing a field a later branch needs.',
      'Adding branch after branch without asking whether the path has stopped being predictable.',
    ]}
  />

  <LessonMindMap content={mindMap} />

</LessonLayout>
